Engine prerequisites for Oracle
    On Oracle database servers (source, staging, target), Delphix needs four key things.

    OS account
            An OS account (often delphix_os) that:
            Belongs to the same primary OS group as the Oracle software owner.
            Has SSH access from the Delphix Continuous Data Engine.
            This account is used by Delphix for:
            Executing local commands (sqlplus, RMAN).
            Running toolkit operations.
            Managing mounts and VDB operations (on targets).
    Toolkit space
            About 1.5 GB of disk space is needed for the Delphix Java-based toolkit.
            The toolkit:
            Is deployed on each managed environment.
            Is used by SSH sessions initiated from the Engine to run Delphix operations.
            Additional free space is recommended for upgrades and maintenance (covered earlier in the lesson for hosts).
    Database account
            A DB account with read-only access to Oracle data dictionary views.
            This account enables JDBC access from the Delphix Engine to:
            Discover objects.
            Read metadata (schemas, tablespaces, etc.).
            Support ingestion operations and monitoring.
    Sudo privileges on targets
            For target environments (where VDBs run), Delphix needs escalated privileges (sudo) for certain OS commands:

            mount, umount – to mount/unmount Delphix NFS storage for VDBs.
            mkdir, rmdir – to manage directories for VDB mount points.
            ps – to inspect processes when managing databases.
            These privileges are essential for provisioning and managing VDBs.

    2.5 Validation with HostChecker
            All prerequisites must be validated using the Delphix HostChecker script on both source and target environments.
            HostChecker confirms:
            OS user and groups are correct.
            Required files, libraries, and permissions exist.
            Connectivity and toolkit paths are valid.

Connecting to the Delphix Continuous Data Engine
        There are multiple ways for administrators to connect to the Engine:

        Web UI (Management Application) via HTTP/HTTPS.
        Initial setup menu via the login screen:
        Click “setup” under the password box to access initial configuration.
        The main configuration is performed using the Server Setup application.

erver Setup application (Engine configuration): 
    The Server Setup application is accessed using the Engine’s IP address or hostname (defined during network configuration), via a specific setup URL (shown in the lesson).

    Within Server Setup, you perform the initial Engine configuration:

    Select Engine type :
        Choose the type of Engine to configure:
        Continuous Data (for database virtualization and data operations).
        Continuous Compliance (for masking/compliance scenarios).
        For this course, the focus is on Continuous Data.

        Set up administrators:
        You define two key admin roles:

        System Administrator (SYSADMIN)
        Engine Administrator (ADMIN)
        For each:

        Provide username and password.
    Provide email addresses used for:
        System alerts.
        Engine alerts.
        Critical notifications (e.g., faults, warnings).
        Important points:

        Use a strong password.
        Keep track of these credentials: if lost, you must contact Delphix Support to reset them.
        
    Run through the Setup Wizard:
        The Setup Wizard guides you through several configuration steps:

        Add NTP Server

        Configure Network Time Protocol so the Engine’s clock is accurate and aligned with other systems.
        Add Network

        Set up network interfaces, IP addressing (static or DHCP), and basic connectivity settings.
        Add Network Security

        Configure firewall/ACL rules, security options, and encryption settings as applicable for your deployment.
        Add Storage

        Define storage devices or volumes the Engine will use for:
        dSources (ingested data).
        VDBs (virtual database storage).
        Typically, this is SAN storage configured as Delphix storage.
        Configure Outbound Connectivity

        Set up connectivity for:
        DNS, NTP, SMTP, LDAP/SAML/SSO, Support upload.
        Ensure the Engine can reach external services needed for:
        Authentication.
        Email alerts.
        Support bundle uploads.
        Configure LDAP and SAML/SSO

        Integrate with directory services and single sign-on if the organization uses them.
        This allows users to log in using corporate identities.
        Configure Network Authentication

        Set up how users and systems authenticate to and through the Engine:
        Possibly Kerberos, LDAP, or other mechanisms, depending on environment.
        Register Engine

        Finalize registration so the Engine is recognized and ready for use in your environment.

HostChecker and BEQ in context of setup
    After the Engine is configured, two things are emphasized:

    HostChecker (environment validation)
        Before adding any environment (source or target) to the Engine:
        Run HostChecker on that host.
        HostChecker:
            Checks OS user configuration.
            Verifies toolkit directories and permissions.
            Confirms required libraries (.so files) are present and accessible.
            Produces warnings if something is missing or misconfigured.
            This reduces setup errors when you later link sources or provision VDBs.

        Bequeath (BEQ) protocol:
            Bequeath (BEQ) is now the default and preferred way for Delphix to connect to Oracle.
            BEQ characteristics:
            No network listener required for those local connections.
            Oracle internally spawns a dedicated server process per client.
            Used for local connections where client and database instance are on the same host.
        In the context of setup:

            sqlnet.ora must allow BEQ connections (as covered earlier in the requirements).
            If OCI binaries are present, HostChecker can test BEQ connectivity.
            HostChecker will only post BEQ warnings/failures if:
            Valid database credentials are supplied.
            TCP connection works (for its test cases).
Putting it together: practical flow
    In practice, Engine Prerequisites and Setup look like this:

    On each Oracle host (source and target):
    Create and configure delphix_os (or equivalent) OS user.
        Ensure toolkit directory and space are available.
        Provide a DB account with read-only data dictionary access.
        Configure sudo for required OS commands (especially on targets).
        Deploy and start the Delphix Engine (via OVA/AMI, covered in another section).

    Access the Server Setup application:

        Choose Engine type (Continuous Data).
        Create SYSADMIN and ADMIN accounts with emails and strong passwords.
        Walk through the Setup Wizard:
        NTP, network, security, storage, outbound connectivity, LDAP/SAML, authentication, registration.
        Run HostChecker on each environment:

        Fix any warnings/failures (permissions, libraries, BEQ settings, etc.).

    Once prerequisites pass HostChecker:

        Add source, staging, and target environments in the Engine UI.
        Proceed to link Oracle databases, create dSources, and provision VDBs.

